        <property name="wrap">none</property>
        <layout manager="place">
          <property name="anchor">nw</property>
          <property name="height">600</property>
          <property name="width">600</property>
        </layout>
      </object>
    </child>
  </object>
  <object class="pygubu.builder.widgets.dialog" id="helpDialog">
    <property name="height">600</property>
    <property name="maxsize">600|600</property>
    <property name="minsize">600|600</property>
    <property name="resizable">none</property>
    <property name="title" translatable="yes">MDMT - Relationship Extraction Documentation</property>
    <property name="width">600</property>
    <child>
      <object class="pygubu.builder.widgets.tkinterscrolledtext" id="helpText">
        <property name="padx">10</property>
        <property name="pady">10</property>
        <property name="state">disabled</property>
        <property name="text" translatable="yes">Relationship Extraction Module Documentation
=========================================================
GENERAL
=========================================================
The relationship extraction module analyzes text and PDF documents to identify entities (such as people, organizations, locations) and the relationships between them. It uses natural language processing techniques through NLTK (Natural Language Toolkit) to extract these relationships and can generate network graphs visualizing the connections.

=========================================================
INPUT
=========================================================
Select Input Directory:
Click the ellipsis icon to select a directory containing your text (.txt) and PDF (.pdf) files. The module will process all such files in the directory, including in subdirectories.

Select Output Directory:
Click the ellipsis icon to select a directory where the results will be saved. You can use the same directory as the input if desired.

=========================================================
PROCESSING MODE
=========================================================
NLTK Processing Settings:
Choose from available processing modes:
  * standard: Basic entity extraction (faster)
  * detailed: More thorough processing (slower but may find more relationships)

=========================================================
ENTITY TYPES
=========================================================
Select Entity Types:
Choose which types of entities to extract relationships for. Available types include:
  * PERSON: People's names
  * ORGANIZATION: Organizations, companies, etc.
  * GPE: Geopolitical entities (countries, cities, states)
  * LOCATION: Non-GPE locations
  * DATE: Dates
  * TIME: Times
  * MONEY: Monetary values
  * PERCENT: Percentages
  * FACILITY: Buildings, airports, highways, etc.

You can select multiple types by holding Ctrl (or Cmd on Mac) while clicking.

=========================================================
OPTIONS
=========================================================
Extract and save document text:
When enabled, the module will save the extracted text content of each document to a separate file in the output directory.

Generate relationship network graphs:
When enabled, the module will create network graphs visualizing the relationships between entities. It will generate:
  * A full network graph showing all relationships
  * Individual graphs for each entity type (e.g., all PERSON relationships)

=========================================================
OUTPUT
=========================================================
The module produces:
  * relationships.csv: A CSV file containing all extracted relationships with columns for source entity, target entity, relationship type, source sentence, and source file
  * [filename]_text.txt: (Optional) Extracted text from each document
  * relationship_network.png: (Optional) Full network graph visualization
  * [entity_type]_network.png: (Optional) Network graphs for each entity type

You can open the CSV file in spreadsheet applications for further analysis, and use the generated graphs in reports or presentations.</property>
        <property name="wrap">word</property>
        <layout manager="place">
          <property name="anchor">nw</property>
          <property name="height">600</property>
          <property name="width">600</property>
        </layout>
      </object>
    </child>
  </object>
</interface>
